  "id": "Compound::DB00200 - Gene::6948",
  "verbalization": "Hydroxocobalamin, a form of cobalamin, is implicated in the relationship with Tcn2 through a series of molecular interactions that ultimately target the cobalamin-binding pathway. The evidence suggests that Hydroxocobalamin binds to two distinct molecules, Cubn and Mmachc, both of which participate in the cobalamin binding process. These molecules are, in turn, recognized by Tcn2, indicating a direct or indirect involvement of Tcn2 in the cobalamin-binding pathway. The consistent scores across the three paths suggest a robust, mechanistically plausible link between Hydroxocobalamin and Tcn2, particularly through the shared role in cobalamin binding. The presence of multiple binding sites and the involvement of Tcn2 in this process supports the hypothesis that Hydroxocobalamin exerts its effects by modulating the cobalamin-binding activity, potentially through the regulation of the Tcn2 protein. This integrated evidence underscores a clear, biologically relevant mechanism by which Hydroxocobalamin may influence Tcn2, aligning with the expectations of a mechanistic analyst.",
